<!-- Frequently Asked Questions Under About -->
<section class="sec sec--band" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap stack">
    <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;text-align:center">
      <p class="eyebrow">Common Questions</p>
      <h2>Questions &amp; Answers</h2>
      <p class="muted">Clear, honest answers to the questions families ask most often.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="max-width:860px;margin:var(--sp2) auto 0;width:100%">
      <details>
        <summary>How much is the compensation?</summary>
        <p>Compensation amounts are set by federal statute: $100,000 for Downwinders, On-Site Participants, and Uranium Workers; and $50,000 (or medical expense reimbursement) for Manhattan Project waste claimants. Federal compensation is tax-free.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>My parent has already passed away. Can our family still file?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Surviving spouses, children, grandchildren, and parents can file on behalf of a deceased relative who met the exposure and illness criteria. This is one of the most common ways claims are submitted.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which years of residency qualify for Downwinders?</summary>
        <p>For downwinders, at least one year of physical presence between January 21, 1951 and November 6, 1962, OR the entire month of July 1962 (June 30 to July 31, 1962). New Mexico claimants qualify starting from September 24, 1944. Uranium workers qualify for employment between 1942 and 1971.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the statutory deadline?</summary>
        <p>Claims must be filed with the Department of Justice on or before December 31, 2027. Because historical document retrieval often takes several months, starting early is essential.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if our claim was previously denied?</summary>
        <p>Many denials occur simply because of documentation gaps—such as an incomplete pathology report or missing residency record—rather than true ineligibility. We review denied claims and resubmit them with complete supporting evidence.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How long does federal processing take?</summary>
        <p>Department of Justice processing generally takes six to twelve months from the date of a complete filing. An expedited path exists for documented terminal diagnoses.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

